Progress status for this period: regressing. This was computed from strengthIndex by a fixed rule, so treat it as settled and use progressiveOverload.notes to explain it with the specific numbers. The rule: regressing if any muscle group's index dropped 3+ points, otherwise on_track if any rose 3+ points, otherwise stalling; changes are measured against previousIndex, or against 100 (the user's first recorded level) when previousIndex is null.

Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. Only make physiological claims that are well established — for example, do not claim that training one muscle group raises hormone levels enough to drive muscle growth elsewhere. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "Your strength dipped this week — focus on recovery and consistency to bounce back.",
  "summary": "This was a challenging week. You got 28 sets in across all major muscle groups, which shows solid commitment, but strength indicators slipped across your three biggest movers — chest, back, and legs — each dropping noticeably from last period. The good news is your shoulders, biceps, and triceps held their ground perfectly, and your overall tonnage of over 10,700 kg demonstrates you're still putting in real work. At 28 years old with a healthy BMI of 23.5, your body is well-positioned to recover and come back stronger — this kind of short-term dip is normal and manageable with the right adjustments.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "notes": "Strength is regressing this period. Chest dropped from 106 to 101 (down 5 points), back dropped from 104 to 100 (down 4 points), and legs dropped from 108 to 102 (down 6 points) — all three exceeding the 3-point regression threshold. Shoulders held flat at 103, biceps held flat at 102, and triceps held flat at 104. The compound, multi-joint movements driving the big muscle groups (chest, back, legs) are all declining simultaneously, which strongly suggests a systemic cause such as accumulated fatigue, insufficient recovery, or a dip in sleep or nutrition — rather than a technique issue with any single lift. The last period's note flagged the need to watch recovery, and this data suggests that warning was warranted.",
    "status": "regressing"
  },
