      "observation": "Legs account for 22.4% of volume split with 6 sets, which is on the lower end for a muscle-building goal where legs typically benefit from higher relative volume. Combined with the largest strength drop this period (down 6 index points), this group may benefit from prioritized attention."
    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Biceps",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Biceps receive only 4% of total volume (3 sets), which is the lowest share of any group. While biceps are partially trained through back work, dedicated volume is low relative to triceps at 8.4% and may create a minor anterior-to-posterior arm imbalance over time."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": true,
  "deloadReason": "Three major muscle groups (chest, back, and legs) all regressed simultaneously by 4 to 6 strength index points, which is a clear signal of accumulated systemic fatigue. A short deload — reducing load or volume by roughly 40 to 50% for the next period — will allow your nervous system and muscles to recover fully, setting you up to return to and surpass your previous strength levels.",
  "actionItems": [
    "Prioritize a deload this coming period: keep your movement patterns and frequency the same, but reduce weights to around 50 to 60% of your usual load and aim for 2 to 3 sets per muscle group rather than your normal volume.",
    "Audit your sleep and nutrition this week — simultaneous drops across chest, back, and legs almost always have a systemic cause. Aim for 7 to 9 hours of sleep and ensure you are eating enough protein (roughly 1.6 g per kg of bodyweight, so around 115 g per day at your current weight) to support recovery.",
    "After the deload, reintroduce leg volume gradually — consider adding a set or two to legs over the following two periods, as they showed the steepest drop and currently sit at the low end of volume for a muscle-building goal.",
    "Consider adding one dedicated biceps set per session in the next full-training period to bring biceps volume closer in line with triceps and support balanced arm development."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "Strength regressed across chest (106 to 101), back (104 to 100), and legs (108 to 102) simultaneously, signaling systemic fatigue; a deload is recommended before resuming progressive overload."
}
